WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.640 --> 00:00:06.700
We’re seeing for the first time in many
decades a pandemic of this kind,

00:00:06.700 --> 00:00:10.800
and that’s bringing out also the fact that stigmatization

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:13.880
can be a huge problem in disease.

00:00:13.880 --> 00:00:15.000
You've seen it in Nepal,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.410
you've seen it in a couple of European countries.

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:19.620
You've seen it in Korea and Japan.

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:24.939
Countries like Italy and America,
you see a different kind of stigmatization,

00:00:24.940 --> 00:00:29.360
which is a lot of it is directed towards the Chinese community.

00:00:29.360 --> 00:00:34.559
There are a few things that are particular
to perhaps South Asian countries.

00:00:34.559 --> 00:00:38.365
Slum areas, for example, are considered a
hotbed of disease.

00:00:38.365 --> 00:00:40.640
You know, right in the beginning of the pandemic

00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.500
in Bombay, you had Dharavi,

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:44.250
which is one of Asia's largest slums.

00:00:44.250 --> 00:00:47.100
The government frontline health workers,

00:00:47.100 --> 00:00:50.020
when they went back to their homes in different neighborhoods,

00:00:50.020 --> 00:00:54.160
they were asked to leave because
they knew they were working in Dharavi.

00:00:54.160 --> 00:00:57.000
We saw that healthcare workers were attacked in Haiti.

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.160
It happened in Nepal, in Iraq.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:06.720
A 60 year old man who was sort of put in quarantine
because he had some symptoms, he tested positive.

00:01:06.720 --> 00:01:10.060
He went into sort of institutional care nearby.

00:01:10.060 --> 00:01:12.990
He came back to, essentially, what I was told

00:01:12.990 --> 00:01:17.479
was an empty house, and a kind of deserted neighborhood.

00:01:17.480 --> 00:01:19.920
I was told that he actually committed suicide.

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:25.920
When you look at past diseases like leprosy and HIV,

00:01:25.920 --> 00:01:28.880
I think those are much more stigmatized diseases.

00:01:28.880 --> 00:01:33.520
Leprosy, you know, sort of the archetypal
stigmatized disease,

00:01:33.520 --> 00:01:36.500
where the associations are so ancient

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:39.520
Even though you don't have much leprosy today

00:01:39.520 --> 00:01:42.520
it still has that lingering association.

00:01:42.520 --> 00:01:45.500
HIV is more recent and it’s slightly different.

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:48.460
because HIV is associated originally with homosexuals,

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:52.509
sex workers, those who are considered the vulnerable groups.

00:01:52.509 --> 00:01:56.510
There's a certain amount of sort of moral
judgment attached to that.

00:01:56.510 --> 00:01:58.780
Stigmatization was such a problem,

00:01:58.780 --> 00:02:00.890
the epidemic wasn’t being dealt with.

00:02:00.890 --> 00:02:03.780
Perhaps most relevant from a public health perspective,

00:02:03.780 --> 00:02:09.560
we've seen in past outbreaks that if they fear or they anticipate

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:12.650
that they're going to be ostracized or stigmatized

00:02:12.650 --> 00:02:15.760
in some way, a lot of people hide their disease.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:17.760
You're not getting the health outcomes because

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:20.140
people aren't adhering to treatment perhaps.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.370
Or delaying care so that, you know, more serious

00:02:22.370 --> 00:02:24.280
symptoms develop and then they can't be treated

00:02:24.280 --> 00:02:25.840
or it's much harder to treat them.

00:02:25.840 --> 00:02:29.040
The best data we have on this is from HIV.

00:02:29.040 --> 00:02:33.435
One analysis they did, from 2011 to 2016,

00:02:33.435 --> 00:02:36.420
They found that one in five people living with HIV

00:02:36.420 --> 00:02:38.290
avoided going to medical facilities

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:42.069
because they feared or anticipated being mistreated.

00:02:42.069 --> 00:02:46.800
Marginalized groups, who are already experiencing stigmatization in society,

00:02:46.800 --> 00:02:49.460
it sort of adds to that condition.

00:02:49.460 --> 00:02:53.660
If you’re for example, a sex worker and you have HIV

00:02:53.660 --> 00:02:55.520
then you're less likely to afford health care.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:02:59.280
You're economically perhaps not as well off,

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:02.100
and then you get the disease.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.980
So, there's what you call a compounding effect.

00:03:05.989 --> 00:03:08.760
All the public health experts and doctors say that

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:10.980
you need to communicate the science very well.

00:03:10.980 --> 00:03:15.460
How transmission actually occurs, what is a virus actually,

00:03:15.460 --> 00:03:20.200
and sort of quash rumors, misinformation, sensationalism.

00:03:20.200 --> 00:03:23.100
Some people point to New Zealand as an example
of good public health messaging.

00:03:23.100 --> 00:03:25.720
"People are not the problem here.

00:03:25.720 --> 00:03:28.340
The virus is the problem. People are the solution.

00:03:28.340 --> 00:03:32.760
There should be no shame and no blame in contracting COVID-19.

00:03:32.760 --> 00:03:39.380
A lot of experts suggest that having the media
also cover cases of recovery

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:42.780
can be really helpful to reduce fear.

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:46.360
Because now we're at least six months into the pandemic,

00:03:46.360 --> 00:03:49.940
I think that we see that there has been some improvement, even in Bombay.

00:03:49.940 --> 00:03:52.520
There is a little less fear

00:03:52.520 --> 00:03:55.720
as you become familiar with the disease and how it spreads.

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:58.420
Not as much as we need, but certainly there's been some improvement.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:02.449
And that's certainly helped in being more perhaps

00:04:02.449 --> 00:04:06.960
rational about, you know, who to avoid when and how.

00:04:06.960 --> 00:04:09.540
It is going to go on for a long time.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:12.100
So we're gonna have to see how this plays out.

